====================================================================
DOMAIN: Communicative Rationality, Universal Pragmatics, and Intercultural Dialogue
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-08
PAPERS_FOUND: 10 total (High: 6, Medium: 4, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the formal-pragmatic dimension of the Dussel-Habermas encounter:
how Dussel's critique targets not merely discourse ethics but the deeper theory of
communicative rationality and universal pragmatics that underlies it. Habermas's
theory posits that communicative rationality is latent in the very structure of
language -- in the validity claims speakers raise to truth, rightness, and
sincerity -- and that an idealized community of communication is presupposed by any
attempt at mutual understanding. Dussel's challenge is that this "ideal communication
community" (drawing on Apel's terminology) already presupposes the inclusion of
interlocutors who share certain background lifeworld conditions; the victims and
excluded peoples of the world system do not in fact participate in this community,
so the formal-pragmatic reconstruction occludes rather than illuminates their
exclusion.

The secondary literature explores this through several sub-debates: (1) whether
Dussel's charge of Eurocentrism is targeted at Habermas's theory per se or at the
conditions for its application; (2) whether the analectic method provides an
alternative epistemological-ethical standpoint or merely supplements formal
pragmatics; (3) whether Fornet-Betancourt's intercultural philosophy project offers
a more workable alternative to universal pragmatics than Dussel's analectic; and
(4) whether Habermas's lifeworld concept, once extended to asymmetric global
conditions, could absorb some of Dussel's challenges.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is the methodological core of the research project. The communicative
rationality / universal pragmatics debate shows exactly where Dussel's critique
bites -- not in a blanket rejection of reason but in a specific challenge to the
formal-pragmatic reconstruction of who gets to count as a valid interlocutor.
The analectics/interculturality angle shows Dussel's positive alternative.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No secondary literature was found that directly analyzes Habermas's (rather than
Apel's) own responses to Dussel on the universal pragmatics issue. The Sorensen
2011 thesis (domain-2) partially addresses this. The Mekonnen and Demenchonok
papers deal with intercultural aspects but are less philosophically rigorous.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should distinguish between Dussel's challenge to the formal grounding
of communicative rationality (Apel-focused) and his critique of the substantive
cultural assumptions of Habermas's lifeworld. Kim's account of analectics and
Schelkshorn's comparison are key for showing the positive alternative.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Transcendental-pragmatic defense (Apel/Mendieta 1996): 2 papers - discourse ethics can address exclusion via Part B
- Analectical alternative (Dussel, Kim): 3 papers - method from the Other outside the community
- Intercultural supplement (Fornet-Betancourt via Demenchonok, Schelkshorn): 2 papers
- Critique of analectic (Irvine): 1 paper - challenges Dussel's claim to go beyond Western ontology
- Historical-descriptive (Mekonnen, Mendieta 2018): 2 papers
====================================================================
}

@article{apel1996discourse,
  author = {Apel, Karl-Otto and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {{`Discourse ethics' before the challenge of `liberation philosophy'}},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1996},
  volume = {22},
  number = {2},
  pages = {1--25},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379602200201},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Apel distinguishes Part A of discourse ethics (transcendental grounding in an ideal communication community) from Part B (application to real historical conditions). He argues that Dussel's critique of the ideal speech situation misunderstands Part A as a sociological description of existing communication rather than a counterfactual normative standard; Part B can and must take account of the material asymmetries Dussel describes. Mendieta's editorial framing contextualizes the Apel-Dussel exchange for English-speaking readers and draws out the tension between transcendental pragmatics and liberation ethics.

  RELEVANCE: This is the central text for understanding how the transcendental-pragmatic tradition attempted to respond to Dussel's challenge that communicative rationality presupposes an already-included community. The Part A/Part B distinction is the key move that Dussel and his defenders argue fails: it defers the material asymmetry question to an application stage while leaving the formal grounding untouched.

  POSITION: Transcendental-pragmatic defense: universal pragmatics is not Eurocentric; its application (Part B) can accommodate the excluded Other.
  },
  keywords = {communicative-rationality, universal-pragmatics, community-of-communication, Apel, liberation-ethics, High},
  abstract = {Mesurant la contribution d'E. Dussel au dialogue concernant la relation entre l'ethique du discours et la philosophie de la liberation, l'A. confronte la conception d'une economie transcendantale, developpee par Dussel, a sa propre conception realiste des rapports conflictuels entre le Nord et le Sud},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mendieta1995discourse,
  author = {Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {Discourse ethics and liberation ethics},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1995},
  volume = {21},
  number = {4},
  pages = {111--126},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379502100406},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mendieta provides a sustained comparison of Habermasian-Apelian discourse ethics and Dussel's ethics of liberation, arguing that while both share a communicative turn and a universalist aspiration, they diverge fundamentally on what communication requires. Discourse ethics grounds normativity in the formal presuppositions of argumentation (validity claims, ideal speech); liberation ethics grounds it in the material conditions of life and the cry of the excluded victim who is outside any actual or counterfactual communication community.

  RELEVANCE: This early article maps the exact fault-lines in the Dussel/Habermas debate at the level of communicative rationality. Mendieta's framing -- that the real disagreement concerns who counts as a valid interlocutor and what communication presupposes -- directly bears on the question of whether universal pragmatics is Eurocentric or merely historically incomplete.

  POSITION: Critical mediation: neither discourse ethics nor liberation ethics alone captures the full structure of communicative rationality under conditions of global asymmetry.
  },
  keywords = {communicative-rationality, discourse-ethics, liberation-ethics, validity-claims, High},
  abstract = {L'A. souligne la complementarite de l'ethique du discours de Apel et de l'ethique de la liberation de Dussel, en ce sens qu'ils redefinissent et rearticulent les frontieres entre les notions du soi, de societe (monde) et de transcendance (Dieu). Tous deux ouvrent la voie a une nouvelle theorie morale en insistant a leur maniere sur les frontieres du discours philosophique},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{schelkshorn2018discourse,
  author = {Schelkshorn, Hans},
  title = {Discourse and liberation: the claims and contexts of two types of universalistic ethics},
  booktitle = {A Matter of Discourse},
  editor = {Nascimento, Am{\'o}s},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {197--218},
  doi = {10.4324/9780429464683-9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schelkshorn argues that discourse ethics and liberation ethics are best understood as two complementary universalisms rather than rivals: discourse ethics formalizes the pragmatic presuppositions of communication, while liberation ethics contextualizes these within the asymmetric material conditions of modernity's underside. The key difference is that Dussel starts from the victim's claim (the material cry of the excluded) and then asks what communication would look like from that standpoint, whereas Habermas and Apel start from the formal structure of argumentation and then derive obligations.

  RELEVANCE: Schelkshorn's comparative analysis of the two types of universalism is the most philosophically precise secondary treatment of the communicative rationality/liberation ethics interface. It directly addresses Dussel's claim that a universal community of communication must be reconstructed from the standpoint of the excluded Other rather than assumed as a formal presupposition.

  POSITION: Complementarist: formal-pragmatic and liberation-based universalisms address different but compatible dimensions of communicative rationality.
  },
  keywords = {universal-pragmatics, communicative-rationality, liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, High},
  abstract = {Discourse ethics continues the European tradition of universal morality at a purified level; it integrates critically the two waves of Enlightenment European history has crossed. The basic principle of discourse ethics is related to the rational adjudication between conflicting interests and claims. Discourse ethics reconstructs and grounds the moral intuition of universal justice with reference to a consciousness of the fallibilistic status of scientific theories and the experience of cultural relativism. Discourse ethics responds to Enrique Dussel’s problem through its demand for practical discourses, where all concerned should solve consensually their conflicting claims and interests. Discourse ethics and the ethics of liberation share the intention to overcome monological universalization through real interactions. The material implications of discourse ethics are immediately obvious under totalitarian conditions. Discourse ethics and liberation ethics try to reconstruct the anticipations which are included in moral intuitions.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{dussel1997principles,
  author = {Dussel, Enrique},
  title = {Principles, mediations, and the {``Good''} as synthesis (from {``Discourse Ethics''} to {``Ethics of Liberation''}},
  journal = {Philosophy Today},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {41},
  number = {Supplement},
  pages = {55--66},
  doi = {10.5840/philtoday199741supplement62},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dussel maps his ethics of liberation as a reconstruction that accepts and then goes beyond discourse ethics. He accepts Apel's formal principle (that valid norms require the consent of affected participants) but argues for two additional principles: a material principle of life (that ethics must begin from the demand to sustain human life) and a feasibility principle (that ethical proposals must be historically realizable). In this framing, discourse ethics captures only the formal-procedural dimension and cannot generate the critical negativity needed when interlocutors are structurally excluded.

  RELEVANCE: This is Dussel's own statement of how he positions his ethics relative to discourse ethics and communicative rationality, making it indispensable for assessing whether Dussel rejects or supplements the Habermasian project. The claim that the community of communication must be expanded via a material principle of life directly challenges Habermas's formal-pragmatic starting point.

  POSITION: Dussel's positive alternative: universal pragmatics needs supplementation by a material principle that names the excluded as the starting point for ethical reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {liberation-ethics, discourse-ethics, material-principle, community-of-communication, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@article{kim2019analectics,
  author = {Kim, David Haekwon},
  title = {Alterity, Analectics, and the Challenges of Epistemic Decolonization},
  journal = {The Southern Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {57},
  number = {S1},
  pages = {37--62},
  doi = {10.1111/sjp.12346},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kim distinguishes between philosophies that are "alterior" (emerging from culturally different but non-subordinated positions) and those that are "analectical" in Dussel's sense (emerging from a politically subordinated, excluded position within the modern world system). The analectical standpoint is not merely culturally other but politically marginalized -- it speaks from outside the existing community of communication. Kim uses this distinction to press for epistemic decolonization in philosophy as a discipline.

  RELEVANCE: Kim's analysis clarifies the analectical method as a response to the problem of who is included in the community of communication. The Dusselian notion that philosophy can only achieve universality by starting from the voice of the excluded -- rather than by enlarging a formal-pragmatic framework -- is here given its most careful English-language treatment. This is directly relevant to the question of whether analectics is an alternative to or a radicalization of universal pragmatics.

  POSITION: Analectical-decolonial: the community of communication must be reconstructed from the standpoint of the excluded Other, not merely formally extended.
  },
  keywords = {analectics, communicative-rationality, epistemic-decolonization, excluded-other, interculturality, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This essay explores some conceptual and diagnostic frameworks to advance epistemic decolonization in the US philosophical profession. A central focus is the distinction between those philosophies of formerly colonized peoples that are culturally alterior or, simply, alterior and those that are analectical in the Dusselian sense of emerging from a subordinated political position. The paper begins by reflecting upon connections between coloniality, the alterior, and the analectical to frame the discussion of epistemic decolonization in the philosophy profession. It then considers a knower's core theoretic reconstruction in the task of epistemic decolonization and identifies some ambiguities in reconciling alterior traditions with analectical theoretical reconstruction. The work of Charles Mills and Enrique Dussel are considered as case studies. The paper concludes with critical reflections on the institutional network of canon, curriculum, and other conceptual and credentialization structures that constitute institutional Eurocentrism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{irvine2011ontological,
  author = {Irvine, Andrew B.},
  title = {An Ontological Critique of the Trans-Ontology of {Enrique Dussel}},
  journal = {Sophia},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {50},
  number = {4},
  pages = {603--624},
  doi = {10.1007/s11841-010-0210-8},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Irvine challenges Dussel's claim that the analectic method enables philosophy to break out of Western ontological closure. He argues that Dussel's appeal to the "revelation of the Other" -- the ethical demand that comes from outside being and inaugurates the analectic movement -- is itself a philosophical gesture that remains intelligible only within a broadly Western ontological horizon. The "trans-ontological" move Dussel requires is, Irvine argues, not available to any philosophy that still uses the conceptual resources of phenomenology (Levinas, Heidegger).

  RELEVANCE: This critique bears on the key question of whether analectics can genuinely provide an alternative to universal pragmatics or whether it is a modification internal to the same conceptual space. If Irvine is right, then Dussel's positive alternative to communicative rationality faces a performative tension: it cannot establish the standpoint of the excluded Other without using philosophical tools it claims to transcend.

  POSITION: Internal critique: the analectic method cannot achieve the trans-ontological standpoint it requires and remains within the Western philosophical tradition it purports to surpass.
  },
  keywords = {analectics, analectic-method, trans-ontology, exteriority, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@incollection{mendieta2018modernity,
  author = {Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {Modernity and postmodernity as challenges to discourse ethics: An ethics of global co-responsibility for an age of suspect foundationalism and rhetorical relativism},
  booktitle = {A Matter of Discourse},
  editor = {Nascimento, Am{\'o}s},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {219--242},
  doi = {10.4324/9780429464683-10},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mendieta argues that discourse ethics faces a double challenge from modernity/postmodernity: on one side, the conditions for unconstrained communication are historically unprecedented and unevenly distributed; on the other, the postmodern dissolution of foundations threatens to undermine the normative ambitions of any universalistic ethics. He proposes an ethics of global co-responsibility that would preserve the universalistic aspirations of discourse ethics while acknowledging the asymmetric material conditions that liberation philosophy highlights.

  RELEVANCE: Mendieta's chapter directly addresses whether discourse ethics can respond to the liberation-philosophy challenge while also resisting postmodern relativism. The "global co-responsibility" frame suggests that universal pragmatics needs not replacement but extension to encompass the asymmetric lifeworld conditions Dussel identifies.

  POSITION: Reformist: discourse ethics can be retained in modified form once it acknowledges global asymmetry -- a partial endorsement of Dussel's critique of universal pragmatics.
  },
  keywords = {discourse-ethics, universal-pragmatics, global-responsibility, liberation-ethics, Medium},
  abstract = {This chapter shows how Karl-Otto Apel’s discourse ethics may be seen as a moral theory that not only discerned and anticipated the critique of modernity by postmoderns, but also formulated an alternative that navigates between the Scylla of relativism and the Charybdis of foundationalism. Apel’s work can be described as the synthesis of three of the most important philosophical currents of twentieth century philosophy. While Apel’s context was post-World War II ravaged Europe, one poised before the dual abysses of decisionistic subjectivism and value neutral scientism, Enrique Dussel’s context was an underdeveloped, anthropologically, economically and politically stunted, colonized and exploited Latin America. If Apel’s quest for a planetary macro-ethics led him to postulate the need for the growth in a planetary public sphere in which many international organizations may adjudicate on world problems, Dussel’s similar quest has led him to discover the structural roots of the growing rift between First and Third World.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{demenchonok2025liberation,
  author = {Demenchonok, Edward V.},
  title = {Latin {American} Philosophy of Liberation and Intercultural Philosophy of Dialogue},
  journal = {RUDN Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {29},
  number = {3},
  pages = {795--818},
  doi = {10.22363/2313-2302-2025-29-3-795-818},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Demenchonok traces how Latin American philosophy of liberation (Dussel, Roig, Cea) generated the intercultural philosophy of dialogue associated primarily with Fornet-Betancourt. He argues that Fornet-Betancourt's "intercultural transformation of philosophy" -- based on a "contextualized and concrete universality" rather than an abstract universalism -- provides an alternative to both Habermasian universal pragmatics and postmodern relativism. The project requires not supplementing but transforming philosophical discourse from within the cultural embeddedness of each tradition.

  RELEVANCE: This article connects the liberation philosophy tradition to the intercultural philosophy movement, showing how Fornet-Betancourt's project offers a more developed version of the interculturality that Dussel's analectics anticipates. For the research project, this is relevant to the question of whether intercultural dialogue is best understood as supplementing or replacing universal pragmatics.

  POSITION: Intercultural transformation: universal pragmatics must be replaced by a culturally embedded dialogue that acknowledges the asymmetric standpoints of participants.
  },
  keywords = {interculturality, intercultural-philosophy, liberation-philosophy, Fornet-Betancourt, universal-pragmatics, Medium},
  abstract = {The research examines the Latin American philosophy of liberation (L. Cea, A. Roig, E. Dussel), from which the intercultural philosophy of dialogue was developed. The dialogue between Russian and Latin American philosophers and the recognition of the philosophy of liberation in Russia are shown. Attention is paid to the discussion about the dialectic of the universal and the culturally-specific, using the example of Latin American philosophy. The article examines intercultural philosophy, which justifies the right to cultural diversity and the normativity of dialogical relations in a culturally diverse world. It analyzes R. Fornet-Betancourt's project on the intercultural transformation of philosophy, which is based on the cultural embeddedness of philosophical traditions.},
  abstract_source = {semantic_scholar},
}

@article{mekonnen2018enlightening,
  author = {Mekonnen, Binyam},
  title = {Enlightening Global Communication: Liberation Philosophy and the Intercultural Stand of the {`Other'}},
  journal = {Ethiopian Journal of the Social Sciences and Humanities},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {13},
  number = {1},
  pages = {25},
  doi = {10.4314/ejossah.v13i1.2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mekonnen traces how liberation theology and Dussel's liberation philosophy reconstruct "intercommunicative" relations from the standpoint of the historical Other. He argues that the enlightenment project of communicative rationality remained Eurocentric by failing to include the colonized Other in the "intercultural stand" -- the recognition that genuine communication requires acknowledging the Other's alterity. Dussel's contribution is to show that the emancipation of the Other is not the endpoint but the starting point of universal communication.

  RELEVANCE: Mekonnen's reading provides a concise account of how liberation philosophy reframes communicative rationality: not by rejecting it but by insisting that its universality is only achievable by beginning from the excluded Other's perspective. This is relevant to the project's question about whether Dussel's critique is reformist or more fundamentally critical.

  POSITION: Reconstructionist from the margins: communicative rationality can achieve universality only if reconstructed from the standpoint of the excluded.
  },
  keywords = {intercultural-communication, liberation-philosophy, communicative-rationality, excluded-other, Medium},
  abstract = {Based on historical records the second half of the twentieth century can be marked as a point of departure for a substantial movement on the topic of liberation across the world; liberation which is contained within a contentious critical popular voice of change on the prevailing and broad political structure of the world. In this period, in different corners of the world people were in protest and war to gain their long lived anguish of being free from the colonial culture of subjugation and exclusion. Although some of the revolutionary movements of this era tended to have economic, socio-cultural and other manifestations, the ultimate anomaly of all these questions were rooted in the ontological and practical premises of liberation, i.e. the enlightenment of the 'Other', the negation of an enforced hegemony, and the reformulation of an intercommunicative just and rational global history of humanity. The key concern of human redemption calls for self-realization as both a particular and universal being by means of methodic politicization. In this paper I sketch the main arguments of liberation theology and Enerique Dussel's liberation philosophy which work for the reconstruction of intersubjective and intercultural communications on the basis of dual redemption in one, which is, emancipation of the 'Other' as an initial and the emancipation of humankind as an end.},
  abstract_source = {core},
}

@incollection{zuniga2021grounding,
  author = {Z{\'u}{\~n}iga M., Jorge},
  title = {Ethics of Liberation and Discourse Ethics: On Grounding the Material Principle of Life},
  booktitle = {Decolonizing Ethics},
  editor = {Allen, Amy and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  publisher = {Penn State University Press},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {107--126},
  doi = {10.1515/9780271090320-007},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Zuniga examines the relationship between the material principle of life in Dussel's ethics and the formal principle derived from discourse ethics/universal pragmatics. He argues that Dussel's material principle is irreducible to Habermasian-Apelian communicative conditions because it grounds normativity in the biological-material conditions for the reproduction of human life, not in the formal presuppositions of language use. This creates a genuine alternative to universal pragmatics, not merely a supplement.

  RELEVANCE: Zuniga's chapter provides the most focused analysis of how the material principle in Dussel's ethics functions as an alternative to the formal-pragmatic grounding of communicative rationality. This is directly relevant to the debate over whether Dussel offers a genuine alternative to Habermasian universalism or merely extends it.

  POSITION: Material-grounding: the ethics of liberation provides a genuinely different foundation than formal pragmatics, grounding norms in life rather than in the presuppositions of communication.
  },
  keywords = {ethics-of-liberation, discourse-ethics, material-principle, universal-pragmatics, communicative-rationality, High},
  abstract = {Chapter examining the relationship between Dussel's material principle of life and discourse ethics, arguing that the material principle of life is irreducible to formal communicative conditions and grounds an alternative to universal pragmatics.},
  abstract_source = {manual},
}
